From f001ebae42d3eb649d873c97978fc31ae1261b23 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jolyon Brown <jolyon@limilo.com>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 12:35:04 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 08/38] Fix exponential walks over chains of conditionals

Three recursive walks over the predecessor graph (four functions, with
the X and Y register variants of the native check) had no visited set,
so in a region of consecutive conditionals they visited a block once
per path to it, and the number of paths doubles with every conditional.
CollectBlocksToDominator also pushed every visit onto its result array,
so the compiler's memory grew without bound (NetHack's explode(), with
140 blocks and 149 million paths between a loop head and its latch,
grew past 8 GB in 20 seconds and was killed); IsDirectLoopPathBlock and
CheckLoopIndexX/YRegisters, which only recurse, took minutes instead.
Their siblings CollectDominatorPath and CollectLoopBody already check
IndexOf before pushing.

The boolean walks return what they returned before wherever the old
code terminated: a block seen again in a failing IsDirectLoopPathBlock
has already been searched without finding the target, and a block seen
again in CheckLoopIndexX/YRegisters has already passed, since a failure
ends the check. A block can also be seen again while it is still on the
recursion stack, but only on a cycle without a loop head, where the old
code never terminated. CollectBlocksToDominator returns the same blocks,
each once and in the order first reached, instead of once per path; its
only caller, HoistCommonConditionalPath, starts it with an empty array
and asks whether any collected block modifies a temporary, which the
duplicates did not change.

Code in which one arm of a conditional holds a long chain of
independent conditionals before the arms join can be affected, at any
optimisation level where the conditions are not constant. A loop body
is the common case, since the latch is the join, but no loop is needed.
bf42402 (d2ea285 here; preserve parameters of address-taken functions)
made the case common: a function called directly with constant arguments
and also through a function pointer used to have the constants folded
into its body at -O2, -Os and -O3, which collapsed such chains.

The test takes the address of three functions whose loop bodies contain
a chain of 24, 18 and 18 independent conditionals. The first reaches
CollectBlocksToDominator and IsDirectLoopPathBlock, the second
CheckLoopIndexXRegisters and the third CheckLoopIndexYRegisters. With
any one of the four checks removed but its Push kept, so that every
revisit also costs memory, the compiler needs a quarter of a gigabyte
to a gigabyte for the file instead of 25 to 40 MB in the configurations
that reach that walk; without the X check it exceeds a 2 GB address
space limit in four of the five native configurations. The chains are
sized so that a compiler without the fix still finishes: it then needs
half a gigabyte for the file, and tens of seconds per native
configuration where the fixed compiler needs about a second. Measured
on one machine at load averages between 3 and 20, that was 8 to 66
seconds against 0.4 to 3.2 per native configuration, and 1 to 10
seconds against 0.15 to 1.3 per bytecode configuration. A regression
shows as compile time and memory, not as a failed assert.

Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
